when you are being portrayed

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.082 line:92%
on "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:50.250 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Is that safe?

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:50.950 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-That is true.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
When I'm on
"Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:54.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's not good news for
the health of the country.

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:59.793
-I love Chris Kattan a great
deal, but I would imagine,

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:01.928 align:start size:94% position:6%
insofar as the people
who played you,

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:04.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
Brad Pitt was probably
the highlight.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:06.733 line:92%
-Yeah. Well, he definitely was

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:11.337
He did a really good job, bu
McKinnon was also spectacular.

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:12.806 line:92%
I mean, she's really amazing

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:15.375 line:92%
I like them both, both of them

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:17.577 line:92%
-You grew up in Brooklyn,

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:22.715 size:84% position:42%
and your family owned
a pharmacy in Brooklyn.

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:26.019
Can you tell me a little bit
about how early did your parents

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
have you and your siblings
working in there?

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:31.024
-Well, you know, we used t
work behind the counter.

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:34.894
But my job was mostly delivering
prescriptions on my Schwinn bike

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:36.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the Bensonhurst section
of Brooklyn

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:39.699 align:start size:94% position:6%
with my little basket,
and the prescriptions in there

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:42.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
going from house to house,
delivering it,

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:45.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
getting a 25-cent
or 15-cent tip.

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:46.272 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
-See, that's the problem
with overregulation.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:51.711
These days they won't let
a 12-year-old bike around drugs.

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:54.814 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:58.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I know your dad
went to Columbia University?

00:01:58.218 --> 00:01:59.486 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Is that right?

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:00.320 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Correct, yes.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.822 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Do you think that
somehow led to your choice

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.324 line:92%
to go into infectious diseases

00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:08.027 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, I think it did
in the sense of not necessaril

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
infectious disease
but medicine in general.

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:13.967 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I had the concep
of service to people

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:17.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
who had a disadvantage
with regard to their health.

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:18.838 line:92%
I was always struck by the fac

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:21.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
that what we were doing
was actually helping people.

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, at a much lower
level than,

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:26.813 align:start size:94% position:6%
obviously,
as a hospital physician.

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:30.216
I was just riding a bicycle, you
know, delivering prescriptions

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:33.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
But there was an element about
that, about helping people

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:37.257
that sort of stood with me
throughout my education.

00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:40.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, I think we all have
or tend to have

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
sort of a short memory insofar
as what the American peopl

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:47.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
can overcome when
faced with a hurdle.

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:51.471
You lived through smallpox
and the vaccination of tha

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:52.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
living in New York
as a very young kid.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.474 size:75% position:38%
What do you remember
about what New York

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:56.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
managed to pull off
during that time?

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, that was
really extraordinary,

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:00.947 size:84% position:42%
what they pulled off --
I believe it was 1947.

00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:05.351
I was a child, and some ma
had gone to Mexico on a vacation

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:08.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then came back to New York
was infected with smallpox

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:14.327
And the city of New York
vaccinated like 6 million people

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:17.564 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a 2 1/2- to 3-week
period of time.

00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:19.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
And one of them
was me and my family,

00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:23.803
because the entire city of New
York got vaccinated that year.

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:24.837 line:92%
And it prevented an outbreak

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:29.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
There were 12 cases, you know,
hospitalizations, 2 deaths

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:30.810 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we didn't have an outbreak
in the city.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.647
It was really extraordinar
public health measure back then.

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:39.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You worked -- You've had
this job for seven presidents.

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:41.821 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I don't necessaril
want to rehash

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:45.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
the last four years,
but was it uniquely differen

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:48.328 align:start size:94% position:6%
having your job
during the last administration

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:52.232 line:92%
than the previous five, I guess?

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:52.765 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.301 size:88% position:44%
It was, Seth, and it was
for a number of reasons,

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
because, I mean, you don't
need me to tell you

00:03:57.937 --> 00:03:59.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we're really
living through a period

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:02.442 align:start size:94% position:6%
of extreme divisiveness
in our country.

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:08.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
And in order to address
and attack a deadly pandemic

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:10.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
everybody's got to
pull together.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:15.088
And what we saw over the las
year was public health issue

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:18.291 align:start size:94% position:6%
that were taking on
a political connotation,

00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:21.261 line:92%
which really just doesn't work

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:22.562 line:92%
You know, it just does not work.

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.464 align:start size:94% position:6%
Everybody's got to be
on the same page.

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:27.800
And when you have wearing a mask
or not wearing a mask

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.404
becomes a political statement,
that was not helpful at all.

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:34.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think there's any lesson
that we learned from this

00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:38.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that when we're fightin
a common enemy,

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:39.412 line:92%
we've got to do it togethe

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:40.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
because we're all
in the same boat

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:43.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we can't be
pulling against each other

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:46.152 size:78% position:39%
-Of course, you know,
the decisiveness --

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:48.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
I should say the divisiveness,
excuse me --

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.423 line:92%
that is still as big a problem

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:52.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
as it was at
the start of this pandemic

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.527 size:81% position:41%
And that leads me now
to this next question.

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:56.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel as though
we are any better prepared

00:04:56.896 --> 00:05:01.467
the next time this happens, be
it in 5 years or 10 years or 20?

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:07.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, if we essentially
acclimate ourselves

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:08.574 size:81% position:41% line:92%
to the lessons learned

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:12.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we don't have
a short-lived corporate memory

00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:13.046 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
we will do well.

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:16.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
We learned some serious lesson
about allowing

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:18.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
our local public health
infrastructure

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:23.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
to essentially become much
weaker than it was decades ago

00:05:23.423 --> 00:05:26.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
We also realize
that we've got to be part of

00:05:26.426 --> 00:05:27.794 line:92%
the international community.

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:31.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
Withdrawing out of
the international scene --

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
The United States
is a global leader

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:36.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
and has been a global leader
in every major pandemic.

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:41.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
A global pandemic requires
a global response.

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:43.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we need to get back
into the action

00:05:43.276 --> 00:05:46.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
and reassume our leadershi
globally.

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:49.415 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Last question --
COVID fatigue is real.

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:52.919
And I know you can't tell us
when we're going back to normal,

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:58.091
but give us one optimistic thing
to leave this interview with

00:05:58.091 --> 00:06:00.093 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, the optimistic thing,
Seth,

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:02.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that this is definitely
going to end.

00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:05.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
We need to hang in there
a bit longer.

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:06.833 line:92%
The saving grace in all of this,

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:10.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
if there's one big success
story, is the vaccines.

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:13.873
If we get the overwhelming
majority of the population

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.274 line:92%
vaccinated, we're going to hav

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.910
a very, very different situation
than we have now.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:21.981
We're going to start to approach
normal, and we will get there.

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:26.586
-In a normal year, do people
ask you about allergies more

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:28.621 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:31.991
I haven't been asked about
an allergy in a long time.

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:32.525 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:33.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
-'Cause, you know,
we were talking here

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:37.230
that it's almost allergy season,
and it feels really quaint now

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.065 line:92%
But I bet two, three years ago

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:41.667 size:88% position:44%
that was all anybody was
ever calling you about.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.102 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-Well, yeah, sort of.

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:44.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're right.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
Interests change
depending on the circumstances

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:50.576 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.945 line:92%
Keep up the important work

